course: dictionary
lesson: 1587
title: Chatty and choosing your register
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A chatty person likes to talk a lot in a friendly way. A chatty letter, email or message is friendly and informal, and full of small news. Context shows you which meaning fits.
explain: Everyone has at least two ways of talking. Informal talk is relaxed and chatty, for mates and family. Formal talk is careful and polite, for teachers, strangers and official letters. The style you choose is called your register.
explain: In a school report, «a bit too chatty in class» means the pupil talks when they should be working. So the same word can be a compliment in one place and a complaint in another.
explain: Switching registers is a skill, not being fake. You can be chatty with friends and careful with a teacher. Ask yourself who you are talking to, then choose your words.
fact: Chatty means friendly and talkative, or friendly and full of small news when it describes writing.
fact: «A diary is personal and chatty» because you write it for yourself, as in «Dear Diary, today was the WORST.»
fact: Register means choosing words to suit your audience: formal language for governors, chattier words for friends.
fact: Chattier is the comparison and chattiest is the top, as in «She is chattier than her brother.»
fact: In stories, a chatty character fills the silence and often lets out secrets.
exercise: «A chatty letter is full of small news and friendly words.» What does chatty mean here? | Long, formal and careful | *Friendly and informal, full of small news | Angry and short | Hidden and secret
exercise: Which register suits a letter to your headteacher? | *Formal, careful and polite | Whispered and quiet | Silly and loud | Chatty, with lots of slang
exercise: Ruby says «I'm chattier with Nan than with the head.» What does chattier mean? | Angrier | Sadder | More careful and formal | *More talkative and relaxed
test: In a school report, «a bit too chatty in class» means the pupil | is the best reader in the class | *talks when they should be working | is off school with a cold | is too shy to speak
test: What is your register? | A kind of school desk | *The style of talk you choose for a situation | The sound your voice makes when you yawn | The number of words you know
exam: Which sentence is written in a chatty register for a friend? | It is with regret that I must inform you. | Please be advised that the trip is cancelled. | Dear Sir, I would be grateful if you could attend. | *Hiya, want to come round after school?
